  <h2>The core does not draw</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@open-slot-ui/core</code> is zero-dependency and has no DOM in it. Every button, readout and panel is a
    small state machine over a signal, and whether a control can be pressed is <em>derived</em> from state rather
    than stored as a flag — which is why a HUD cannot end up with a spin button that is enabled during a spin.
    It also owns the things a game must not get wrong twice: money formatting, the bet ladder, the autoplay
    limits, the jurisdiction switchboard, and the never-throwing validator that reads your spec.
  </p>

  <h2>A binding draws, and owns no rules</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@open-slot-ui/dom</code> renders the HUD as real markup — the ids and classes a casino skin already
    expects — and wires each element to a control. It ships <strong>no stylesheet</strong>: you pass the one your
    game wears. Everything in the <a href="/gallery/">gallery</a> is that binding, dressed in the example's skin.
  </p>

  <h2>One object decides what exists</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>UISpec</code> is the whole configuration: which parts exist at all, the bet ladder, the autoplay
    options and limits, the currency, the locale dictionary, the menu and its rules. A value the library cannot
    use is reported and dropped — never thrown — so a bad config degrades the HUD instead of taking the game
    down with it.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`import { mountDomHud } from '@open-slot-ui/dom';

const hud = mountDomHud({
  currency: { code: 'USD', symbol: '$', display: 'symbol', position: 'prefix', decimals: 2 },
  betLadder: { levels: [0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10], index: 3 },
  autoplay: { options: [10, 25, 50, 100], lossLimits: [5, 20, 50], winLimits: [10, 20, 75] },
  hud: { features: { buyFeature: true, history: true, info: true } },
  rules,                       // blocks — see the menu-blocks gallery
}, {
  skin: { href: '/ui.css', font: { family: 'icomoon', src: '/icons.woff2' } },
  features: [{ id: 'free-spins', name: 'Free Spins', variant: 'buy', cost: 100 }],
  onBuy: (id, cost) => { /* your game starts the feature */ },
});`}</code></pre>

  <h2>The round, in three phases</h2>
  <p>
    A spin is not a boolean. The round button is idle, then <em>in flight</em> — dimmed, but not a stop button,
    because there is nothing to stop until the server has answered — and only then <em>slam-to-stop</em>. That is
    one button in three phases, never two buttons at once; the
    <a href="/gallery/#control-round">round-button story</a> walks them.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`ui.spin.busy();        // the player pressed it; the request is out
ui.spin.stopState();   // the answer arrived — now it can be slammed
ui.spin.idle();        // the reels have landed

ui.on('spinRequested', () => game.spin());
ui.on('skipRequested', () => game.slam());`}</code></pre>

  <h2>The rules are content, not code</h2>
  <p>
    The info window is written in <a href="/gallery/blocks/">blocks</a> — headings, tables, symbol tables, reel
    grids, meters, timelines — which both renderers understand. They can be written as objects or parsed from a
    markup file a writer edits, and they are <em>audited</em>: every mode the game declares must have its own
    section stating its RTP, its max win and its price, or the window says so where a reader will see it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tests hold on to</h2>
  <p>
    Every control reports its state, interactability and bounds through <code>window.__OPENUI__</code>, so an
    end-to-end suite asserts on facts rather than on pixels. The cases that exist today are listed, straight from
    the suite files, on the <a href="/gallery/cases/">test-cases page</a>.
  </p>
